\fs18 \cf0 Abstract\'97Campus print facilities experience intense operational surges during assignment deadlines and examination cycles. Traditional in-person submission models\'97relying on physical queues, shared USB flash drives, or informal messaging channels\'97suffer from throughput bottlenecks, malware transmission risks, and opaque order tracking. This paper details Xeroxic, an end-to-end web platform engineered to modernize campus document intake, automated pricing, payment verification, and administrative queue management. To operate reliably within resource-bounded cloud environments, the system decouples control-plane operations from heavy document payloads: a Node.js serverless API validates file eligibility and grants scoped authorization, while raw document binaries stream directly between client browsers and Supabase object storage via time-limited pre-signed URLs. This direct-to-storage strategy circumvents the 4.5 MB request payload ceiling common to serverless runtimes. Security controls incorporate role-based authorization across student and administrative tiers, memory-hard scrypt credential hashing with synthetic dummy executions to eliminate user-enumeration side channels, tamper-evident HttpOnly session cookies, sliding-window rate limiting, and server-side HMAC-SHA256 verification of Razorpay payment callbacks. A comprehensive automated test suite consisting of 84 test cases verified functional state machines, perimeter defenses, and fallback routines. We analyze the architectural boundaries, evaluate the empirical test coverage, and outline operational requirements for campus-wide pilot deployment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Index Terms\'97campus printing services, serverless computing, pre-signed upload URLs, role-based access control, cryptographic verification, web application security.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 I. INTRODUCTION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 Academic printing remains an indispensable everyday reality across collegiate institutions. Even in modern digitized universities, course coordinators, laboratory instructors, and administrative offices routinely mandate physical submissions for term papers, lab records, engineering drawings, and dissertations. Yet the physical counter experience has remained remarkably archaic. When hundreds of students converge upon a campus xerox center before morning submission deadlines, the counter becomes an overwhelming bottleneck. Document exchange typically takes place via unvetted USB flash drives plugged into shared counter terminals\'97a recognized vector for malware propagation\'97or through uncompressed chat messages where print attributes (color balance, paper weight, single versus double-sided orientation) are communicated verbally amidst chaotic counter noise. Pricing disputes, lost printouts, and uncertainty regarding queue positions are chronic daily friction points.\
Designing a web-based management layer to streamline this intake encounters a fundamental cloud infrastructure bottleneck: serverless payload limits. Modern serverless platforms, including Vercel and AWS Lambda, deliver cost-effective automated elasticity well suited to intermittent campus demand, but impose rigid execution boundaries. Specifically, request body sizes are hard-capped between 4.5 MB and 6 MB. A typical academic submission\'97such as a 40-page scanned engineering report or an image-dense project portfolio\'97routinely spans 15 to 40 MB. Funneling raw file bytes through intermediate serverless function endpoints triggers severe memory pressure, prolonged connection timeouts, and HTTP 413 payload-size termination faults. Xeroxic resolves this structural limitation by strictly isolating application metadata from binary payloads: the API verifies identity and mints short-lived pre-signed storage tokens, allowing client browsers to upload document binaries directly into private object storage.\
This work makes three primary contributions. First, it formalizes the campus counter workflow into an auditable five-stage state machine (REQUEST_RECEIVED -> ACCEPTED -> PRINTING -> READY -> COMPLETED) paired with memorable order identifiers to streamline physical pickups. Second, it implements an authorized direct-to-storage upload and metadata-confirmation protocol that completely eliminates binary proxying through serverless compute functions. Third, it evaluates the implementation across an 84-point automated test suite, systematically analyzing role boundaries, pricing integrity, payment signature verification, and scale limits, while defining a concrete governance framework for campus-wide adoption.\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\f0\i\b\fs20 \cf0 II. PROBLEM DEFINITION AND REQUIREMENTS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 An effective campus printing portal must reconcile distinct operational expectations across student patrons and print center staff. Students require an intuitive mobile-friendly workflow: uploading eligible coursework files, configuring print parameters (color vs. grayscale, single-sided vs. double-sided, binding options, copy counts), previewing server-verified price quotes, and tracking order readiness in real time. Shop technicians, conversely, need an auditable, prioritized queue where incoming paid jobs can be inspected and transitioned through standard production milestones without accidental cross-user document leakage. Crucially, pricing logic must reside entirely on the server; client-submitted totals are treated strictly as untrusted requests and recalculated against authoritative rate records.\
Tenant isolation and data protection are fundamental constraints. Academic submissions contain sensitive intellectual property, graded assessments, and personal student information. File metadata records explicitly link each document to an authenticated owner identifier and a scoped object path. System routes enforce strict role separation among students, shop administrators, and super-administrators, rejecting unauthorized role escalation. To reduce the file-intake attack surface, the upload validation pipeline accepts only standardized academic file formats (PDF, DOCX, PPTX, TXT, and common raster imagery), actively rejecting executable binaries, scripts, and active web formats.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa20\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

