\fs20 \cf0 III. RELATED WORK AND DESIGN RATIONALE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 Function-as-a-Service (FaaS) environments offer compelling economic advantages for campus services because execution resources automatically scale to absorb peak traffic during midterms and finals while scaling to zero cost during academic breaks [1], [2]. However, handling high-volume binary data within containerless runtimes has long been recognized as an architectural anti-pattern. Streaming heavy multipart payloads through short-lived function runtimes exhausts ephemeral container memory and triggers gateway timeouts [3]. Modern object storage services, including Supabase Storage and Amazon S3, resolve this tension by providing pre-signed URL primitives [4]. By generating time-delimited, cryptographically signed upload and retrieval URLs on the server, applications delegate high-bandwidth data transfers directly to high-throughput cloud storage engines.\
Traditional campus print management platforms (such as CUPS and PaperCut) operate primarily as network printer spoolers requiring dedicated local servers, domain authentication, and vendor-specific print drivers. In typical college copy shops, however, multi-function copiers are directly connected to local operator PCs rather than enterprise networks. Xeroxic deliberately targets the application-layer workflow\'97file ingestion, price calculation, digital payment, and queue tracking\'97leaving the final physical print command to counter operators. This lightweight approach avoids complex driver integration while delivering comprehensive end-to-end order accountability.\
Security mechanisms throughout the platform follow defense-in-depth principles rather than superficial interface validation [5]. Student credentials are protected via the scrypt key derivation function, whose memory-hard architecture offers robust resistance against specialized ASIC and GPU brute-force attacks [7]. User sessions utilize signed, HttpOnly cookies with SameSite=Lax flags, preventing script-based token extraction and reducing cross-site request forgery risks. For financial transactions, Razorpay's server-verification model was implemented: checkout signatures are validated via HMAC-SHA256 digests over concatenated gateway identifiers [8], [10], evaluated with timing-safe comparison primitives [9] to neutralize timing side-channel attacks.\
Alternative architectures were evaluated during system design. A traditional monolithic backend buffering multipart file uploads in server RAM was rejected due to vertical scaling costs and provider timeout thresholds. Storing document content directly as binary fields within database records was dismissed due to storage bloat and slow query performance. A fully public storage bucket was considered unacceptable because academic documents must remain strictly confidential. Xeroxic adopts an optimal hybrid paradigm: the Node.js serverless API retains exclusive ownership over authorization, pricing, and metadata indexing, while the underlying object store manages secure, isolated binary storage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 IV. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb20\sa60\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\f0\i\b\fs16 \cf0 Fig. 1. Xeroxic separates authorization and metadata processing from the intended direct object-store transfer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 A. Application tiers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The presentation tier is implemented with standard semantic HTML5, responsive CSS3, and modular vanilla JavaScript. It delivers distinct, role-tailored interfaces for students, shop administrators, and super-administrators without heavy client-side framework overhead. The control tier is a single Node.js request router that can run behind Vercel routing or through a local HTTP server. It parses requests, verifies session tokens, enforces route roles, calculates order totals, and calls persistence and storage adapters. The persistence adapter supports JSON-backed local storage for development and MongoDB Atlas when configured. Document content is intentionally represented as an object path and metadata record rather than as a database binary field.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 B. Direct upload and retrieval\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 For a direct upload, the browser first calls /api/files/prepare-upload with filename, byte size, and MIME type. The server normalizes the filename, enforces an extension allowlist, rejects declared sizes exceeding 50 MB, and constructs a tenant-scoped path under students/\{userId\}/. It then requests a signed upload URL from Supabase Storage. Following the browser's direct transfer, /api/files/confirm-upload verifies that the reported path begins with the authenticated student's prefix and persists only the metadata. The intended effect is to keep document bytes entirely out of the serverless function, bypassing the execution payload ceiling noted in [3].\
For retrieval, a file record is located by identifier and access is allowed only to its owner or a recognized staff role. If the record holds an object-store path, the service generates a temporary signed download URL with a one-hour expiry window; otherwise, the local-file route serves the stored fallback. Signed URLs serve as a delivery mechanism rather than a substitute for authorization: access validation strictly precedes URL issuance [4].\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 C. Order and assignment workflow\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 An order encapsulates user identity, item specifications, pricing attributes, total amount, payment status, and fulfillment state. The backend derives line amounts as rate x pages x copies and sums submitted items against authoritative pricing schedules. A paid request becomes visible to counter staff, who advance the workflow across REQUEST_RECEIVED, ACCEPTED, PRINTING, READY, and COMPLETED. To assist campus communication, an assignment hub allows staff to publish syllabus sheets and lab manuals for student viewing and printing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 D. Trust boundaries and failure behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Figure 1 highlights the critical operational trust boundaries. The student browser is treated as an untrusted client: requested page counts, filenames, payment states, and URL parameters are all subject to server-side validation. The API manages server secrets and enforces business logic. Object storage hosts binary payloads under strict bucket policies to prevent cross-tenant enumeration. Razorpay provides independent financial settlement, while the internal database tracks workflow progression. This separation makes failure modes predictable: a storage interruption cannot create a ghost file record, and an unverified payment cannot dispatch a print request.\
The codebase includes development fallbacks to support offline local testing. While beneficial during rapid prototyping, these fallbacks alter assurance levels. For example, a simulated order identifier demonstrates that the HMAC verification route handles expected string shapes, but cannot prove bank settlement. A production deployment must disable local simulation paths and fail closed whenever external verification services are unavailable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 V. IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 A. Authentication and session handling\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 User credentials are salted with 16 cryptographically random bytes and hashed using Node's synchronous scrypt interface (N = 16384, r = 8, p = 1, 64-byte key). A synthetic dummy scrypt calculation executes whenever an unregistered email is queried, equalizing response times to prevent account-enumeration timing leaks. Sessions utilize base64url payloads signed with HMAC-SHA256, encapsulating user identifier, name, email, role, and a seven-day expiration. Tokens are transmitted within HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookies, with the Secure flag enforced in production environments. Authentication endpoints are guarded by an in-memory sliding-window IP rate limiter to mitigate automated brute-force attacks.\
API responses apply no-store cache directives and defense-in-depth security headers, including X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN, Referrer-Policy, and HSTS. These controls follow established web security practices [5] to protect students against common browser-level attack vectors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 B. Payment verification\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The payment module provides both a local testing route and a live Razorpay integration. In the Razorpay route, /api/create-order generates a gateway order from a server-verified total. Upon completion, /api/payments/verify reconstructs HMAC-SHA256(order_id || "|" || payment_id, key_secret) and evaluates it against the received signature using crypto.timingSafeEqual. Only a cryptographically verified signature transitions an order to PAID, and the internal order must strictly match the active student session.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 C. Deployment configuration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The persistence layer implements a flexible repository pattern, supporting atomic JSON files for local development and MongoDB Atlas when MONGODB_URI is supplied. When deployed to Vercel, the local adapter seeds temporary data into /tmp, functioning as an ephemeral demonstration environment. Production deployment requires durable database storage, an immutable SESSION_SECRET, private bucket policies, and active webhook monitoring. Storage adapters include fallback mechanisms for development, but production instances should fail closed on storage errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 D. Data model and consistency\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The data model is lightweight and document-centric. User records maintain identity, role, salt, and password hash. File records track file identifiers, owner IDs, original names, storage keys, bucket names, sizes, and MIME types. Order records bind user identity, item attributes, computed totals, payment methods, and status timestamps. Print-request records emerge upon successful payment, serving as actionable queue items. Notifications reference order identifiers to keep pending and paid status displays synchronized.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 E. Operational sequence\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A standard print request follows a predictable sequence. The student signs in, selects a valid file, and receives upload authorization. The browser transfers the file directly to object storage and confirms metadata. The student specifies print attributes, prompting the server to calculate the authoritative total and initiate a payment order. Following payment, the server validates the signature, marks the order PAID, and queues the job. Staff inspect the file via authorized download links and advance the order state. The student observes live status updates without ever gaining direct access to underlying storage buckets or peer records.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 VI. SECURITY ANALYSIS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 The primary authorization concern in multi-tenant portals is Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR), where an adversary modifies an order or file identifier to inspect another student's document. Xeroxic mitigates IDOR by validating owner identifiers against the authenticated session across all download, order retrieval, and payment endpoints. Administrative access requires explicit role validation. Automated test cases verify cross-student isolation and student-to-administrator access rejection.\
File-upload threats include malicious executable execution, path traversal, oversized payloads, and persistent storage pollution. Xeroxic normalizes submitted filenames, enforces a strict file-extension allowlist, generates object keys server-side, and rejects confirmation paths that deviate from the user's assigned prefix. Institutional deployments should further augment this perimeter with MIME validation, antivirus scanning, and least-privilege storage bucket policies as recommended by OWASP [6].\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A. Authentication attack surface\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Password hashing, generic error messaging, and IP rate limiting provide layered defense against credential attacks. scrypt increases the computational complexity of offline dictionary attacks [7]. Dummy hashing neutralizes timing differences between valid and invalid usernames. Rate limiting restrains rapid automated login attempts. HttpOnly cookies prevent client-side script token theft, while SameSite=Lax mitigates basic cross-site request forgery. Administrative portals should further enforce multi-factor authentication (MFA) in production.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 B. Authorization and storage attack surface\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Resource identifiers serve strictly as references, never as authorizations. Signed-URL routes verify record ownership and staff permissions before requesting storage tokens. Upload confirmations verify path prefixes before persisting metadata. Similarly, order verification requires an exact match between the session identity and the order's owner identifier. Enforcing these checks at the API layer ensures access control remains intact regardless of external storage permissions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 C. Payment attack surface\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Payment verification resists client-side tampering by computing signatures with a server-held secret. The system rejects incomplete payloads, mismatched signatures, and attempts to verify an order belonging to another student. Production deployments should further incorporate webhook replay prevention, unique transaction tracking, settlement reconciliation, and alert mechanisms for indeterminate payment states.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 D. Privacy and availability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Academic files frequently contain intellectual property, graded assessments, or sensitive personal data. Role-based access, temporary download links, and private storage reduce unauthorized exposure. File auto-purge routines delete completed document binaries from storage, conserving quota and protecting student privacy. System availability is maintained through clear error propagation, preventing network or service hiccups from creating orphaned print jobs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 VII. EVALUATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 The platform was evaluated through an automated test suite (npm test) executed within the local project environment. The test harness completed 84 named checks across six functional domains with a 100% pass rate. Test categories included session management, static asset delivery, file operations, order tracking, role boundaries, assignment distribution, notification feeds, rate limiting, Razorpay signature validation, direct-upload handshakes, scale limits, and latency targets. The test execution confirms control-flow correctness, route authorization integrity, and graceful fallback handling under the tested environment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A. Test methodology\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The test suite invokes the primary serverless request handler and evaluates HTTP response codes, headers, cookie values, and database records. The testing workflow begins by confirming authentication and static routing, creating a student profile, executing fallback file uploads, and validating download permissions. It then logs into seeded administrator and super-administrator accounts to evaluate role boundaries, dynamic pricing updates, assignment publication, and notification dispatching. Adversarial tests verify HTTP 403 responses on unauthorized administrative access and HTTP 429 responses under excessive login traffic.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb80\sa20\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

