\fs19 \cf0 The evaluation verifies implementation fidelity against concrete test scenarios. Direct-upload routes enforce 50 MB size ceilings and prefix constraints; HMAC verification routes validate matching signatures and reject counterfeit digests; and memorable order ID generators produce clean, human-readable references (such as sumit24001). Production validation should supplement these unit tests with live concurrency stress testing, physical printer handoffs, and real-world network latency benchmarks.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb70\sa30\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 B. Interpretation of results\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A flawless test execution (84 of 84 passing) proves that the application logic adheres strictly to its specification within the local environment. It confirms that route handlers, role middleware, and cryptographic routines behave as intended. However, integration tests using localized fallbacks cannot independently establish cloud storage availability or bank transaction settlements. Transparent reporting of fallback conditions ensures clear differentiation between verified code logic and live field operations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 VIII. LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 Xeroxic currently functions as an application-level print request manager; counter staff manually initiate physical printing after reviewing submitted documents. It does not yet include automated spooler dispatchers to physical printers via IPP or CUPS network daemons. Development fallbacks must be strictly disabled prior to production deployment. Future development will focus on automated ClamAV antivirus scanning prior to staff retrieval, automated duplex page-count analysis via PDF binary parsing engines, and real-time push notifications over WebSockets.\
A phased deployment roadmap is envisioned: Phase 1 conducts production configuration audits (rotating development secrets, deploying managed MongoDB clusters, and enforcing strict Supabase RLS policies). Phase 2 introduces institutional Single Sign-On (SSO) integration via Google Workspace / SAML and automated webhook reconciliation for financial audits. Phase 3 pilots direct IPP spooler bridges within high-volume departmental print labs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 IX. DEPLOYMENT READINESS AND PILOT PROTOCOL\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 Institutional deployment demands disciplined configuration governance. All runtime secrets (SESSION_SECRET, storage service keys, and payment credentials) must be managed through secure platform key stores rather than local configuration files. Object storage buckets must enforce private access control with zero public read permissions, allowing object retrieval solely through time-limited signed URLs. Dependency trees should be locked and monitored for known vulnerabilities.\
A structured campus pilot protocol should initially operate alongside existing counter workflows. Key performance indicators to monitor include queue turnaround times, payment reconciliation accuracy, file intake error rates, and counter operator throughput. Clear incident response procedures must be established for payment discrepancies, file formatting discrepancies, and hardware jams, ensuring that technical dependency faults never disrupt student assignment submissions.\
Finally, governance policies should treat submitted documents as institutional records. Campus policies must define retention windows for completed orders, authorized staff access roles, and formal deletion schedules. After the pilot demonstrates operational stability, printer spooler integrations can be safely introduced within isolated network zones.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs20 \cf0 X. CONCLUSION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa60\pardirnatural\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs19 \cf0 This paper presented Xeroxic, a lightweight, serverless print request management system designed to eliminate congestion and operational friction at campus print facilities. By decoupling control-plane authorization from data-plane binary transfers, the architecture enables high-capacity document uploads without exceeding serverless memory and payload limits. Security-first engineering\'97featuring scrypt password derivation, tamper-proof session tokens, role-based boundary enforcement, and server-side HMAC payment verification\'97safeguards student data and financial transactions. Validated across an 84-point automated test suite, Xeroxic demonstrates how lightweight serverless architectures can effectively modernize academic print services, establishing a dependable foundation for institutional deployment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb100\sa40\pardirnatural\qc\partightenfactor0

